From dbff982a9ba230456d0180ea7fec65a0242fab88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 14:48:06 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Replace albumentations with a vendored augmentation module

DeepLabCut's PyTorch data pipeline subclassed albumentations transforms and
reached into their internals: the per-item apply_to_bbox()/apply_to_keypoint()
hooks, targets_as_params/get_params_dependent_on_targets(), the public
keypoint_scale() and scale() helpers, ElasticTransform's alpha_affine,
PadIfNeeded.PositionType, and the max_/min_ arguments of CoarseDropout. Every
one of those was moved or removed between 1.4.3 and 2.0, and albumentations 2.x
silently accepts the removed keyword arguments and then ignores them, so a
version bump can change the augmentation actually applied without any error.

Replace the dependency with deeplabcut/pose_estimation_pytorch/data/augmentation.py,
which implements just what DeepLabCut uses, with conventions chosen to be
explicit rather than clever:

* images are (H, W, C); keypoints are (N, C) arrays whose first two columns are
  x and y, with any further columns carried through untouched and no keypoint
  ever dropped; bounding boxes are (M, 4), exchanged with Compose in COCO layout
  and held internally as absolute corners;
* every transform samples from an explicit numpy Generator, so a pipeline can be
  made reproducible by passing a seed to Compose;
* transforms expose the parameters they sampled, so annotations are always moved
  by exactly the geometry applied to the image.

Provided: Compose, KeypointParams, BboxParams and the BasicTransform /
DualTransform / ImageOnlyTransform bases, plus HorizontalFlip, Resize,
LongestMaxSize, PadIfNeeded, RandomCrop, Affine, Normalize, ScaleTo01, Equalize,
ToGray, MotionBlur, GaussNoise, ElasticTransform and CoarseDropout.

Verified against albumentations 1.4.1, the version this recipe shipped, by
running both pipelines over the same inputs and diffing image, keypoints and
bounding boxes:

* every deterministic augmentation is identical -- resize with and without
  aspect ratio, longest_max_size, hflip with and without symmetries, normalize,
  scale_to_unit_range, and padding at center/top_left/bottom_right, both alone
  and composed;
* the primitives match individually as well, including Affine across rotation,
  scaling and translation, where image, keypoints and boxes agree to float
  epsilon;
* the stochastic transforms cannot match a different library's RNG stream, so
  they were checked structurally instead. Elastic deformation is the one that
  matters, since it moves keypoints: against a warped bright-dot image the
  vendored implementation places keypoints within 0.2 px of where the pixels
  actually went, against 0.5-1.1 px for albumentations.

Behavioural notes:

* PadIfNeeded translates annotations and never mirrors them into a reflected
  border. albumentations >= 1.4.15 reflects them, turning one annotated instance
  into several.
* KeepAspectRatioResize now scales bounding boxes. It previously returned them
  unchanged, which was correct only because albumentations held boxes in
  normalised coordinates internally; boxes are absolute here.
* CoarseDropout keeps bounding boxes and marks covered keypoints as not visible,
  as before.

The "albumentations" name is left alone where it is a project configuration
value (default_augmenter) so existing projects keep loading.
